---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Precision preview — experimental | Zodiacs.org';
const description = 'An experimental compact-ephemeris runtime, running in your browser on a pack you supply or on a synthetic fixture built in the page. Not the engine behind zodiacs.org charts, and not a product.';
---
<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/developers/precision-preview/"
  noindex={true}
  privateSurface={true}
  noServiceWorker={true}
  noAssistant={true}
>
  <section class="section container container--narrow pp">
    <em class="kicker">Experimental</em>
    <h1 class="display">Zodiacs Precision — experimental preview</h1>

    <p class="pp-lede">
      A second, experimental calculation path. It is not what{' '}
      <a href="/birth-chart/">/birth-chart/</a> uses, it does not load on any
      other page, and nothing here changes a chart anywhere on this site.
      Treat every number on this page as a research output.
    </p>

    <div class="shell pp-warn-box">
      <p><strong>No ephemeris data ships with this.</strong> A real calculation
      needs a coefficient pack you prepare yourself from a kernel you already
      have — the redistribution question for derived coefficients is open and
      unresolved, so nothing derived from a kernel is published here. The
      preparation route is in the package under{' '}
      <code>examples/00-prepare-a-pack.md</code>, and it is tested from a
      clean install.</p>
      <p>Without a pack, the button below builds a <strong>synthetic
      fixture</strong> in this tab: four bodies on circles, with the
      Earth–Moon barycentre pinned at the origin, so the observer runs a
      4,671 km monthly circle of its own. The arithmetic is real and the
      sky is not. Every result
      from it is labelled.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-head">What differs from the ordinary engine</h2>
    <div class="pp-grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>Where the numbers come from</h3>
        <p>The ordinary engine evaluates analytic series in the browser. This
        reads Chebyshev coefficients fitted to a JPL development ephemeris and
        evaluates the polynomial, with the velocity taken as that polynomial's
        analytic derivative rather than a finite difference of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>The reduction</h3>
        <p>Light-time iteration, solar deflection, relativistic aberration,
        IAU 2000 frame bias, IAU 2006 precession, and all 77 published terms
        of IAU 2000B nutation — where the shipped engine's library keeps five
        of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>Two searches, two claims</h3>
        <p>An <strong>empirical</strong> search over apparent longitude of
        date, which finds events and never claims to have found them all; and
        a <strong>validated</strong> search over geometric longitude in a
        fixed ecliptic 23 milliarcseconds from J2000's, which can prove it for
        the loaded pack. They are different quantities and differ by hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>What it does not do</h3>
        <p>No houses, no aspects, no chart. No Δ T: it takes TT. No
        topocentric parallax, no refraction. For Mars outward the kernel has
        no body centre, so what comes back is the planetary-system barycentre
        and says so.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-head">1 · Supply data</h2>
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="pp-row">
        <button id="pp-synthetic-btn" class="btn">Build the synthetic fixture</button>
        <label class="pp-file-label">or choose a pack from your disk
          <input type="file" id="pp-file" accept=".zeph,.bin,application/octet-stream">
        </label>
        <button id="pp-unload" class="btn btn--quiet">Dispose</button>
      </p>
      <p id="pp-pack-state" class="pp-state">Nothing loaded. Every calculation below will be refused until there is.</p>
      <p id="pp-synthetic" class="pp-warn" hidden>Synthetic fixture. Not planetary positions.</p>
      <dl id="pp-info" class="pp-dl"></dl>
      <div id="pp-corrections" class="pp-corrections"></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-head">2 · Places</h2>
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="pp-row">
        <label>Instant (UTC) <input type="text" id="pp-instant" value="2024-06-15T12:00:00Z" spellcheck="false"></label>
        <button id="pp-places" class="btn" data-needs-pack disabled>Compute</button>
      </p>
      <p id="pp-places-state" class="pp-state"></p>
      <div id="pp-places-out" class="pp-out"></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-head">3 · Search, and what the answer claims</h2>
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="pp-row">
        <label><input type="radio" name="pp-mode" value="empirical" checked> empirical, apparent of date</label>
        <label><input type="radio" name="pp-mode" value="geometric"> validated, geometric J2000</label>
      </p>
      <p class="pp-row">
        <label>Body
          <select id="pp-body">
            <option>Sun</option><option>Moon</option><option>Mercury</option><option>Venus</option>
            <option selected>Mars</option><option>Jupiter</option><option>Saturn</option>
            <option>Uranus</option><option>Neptune</option><option>Pluto</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label>Longitude ° <input type="text" id="pp-target" value="100" spellcheck="false"></label>
        <label>Max rate °/day <input type="text" id="pp-rate" value="20" spellcheck="false"></label>
      </p>
      <p class="pp-row">
        <label>From (UTC) <input type="text" id="pp-from" value="2024-01-01T00:00:00Z" spellcheck="false"></label>
        <label>To (UTC) <input type="text" id="pp-to" value="2024-12-31T00:00:00Z" spellcheck="false"></label>
        <button id="pp-search" class="btn" data-needs-pack disabled>Search</button>
        <button id="pp-cancel" class="btn btn--quiet">Cancel</button>
      </p>
      <p class="pp-dim">
        Cancel stops the worker, which is the only way to interrupt a
        calculation already running here — a cancel message cannot be read
        while the worker is inside one, and the mechanism that would work
        across that needs cross-origin isolation this page does not have.
        So cancelling drops whatever is loaded, and you load it again.
      </p>
      <p id="pp-search-state" class="pp-state"></p>
      <div id="pp-search-out" class="pp-out"></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-head">What the page does not do</h2>
    <ul class="pp-list">
      <li>It never downloads a pack. A pack arrives only from the file you choose, and is read in this tab.</li>
      <li>It stores nothing: no cookies, no local storage, no session
      storage, no database, and the site's offline worker is told not to
      cache this route either.</li>
      <li>It sends nothing anywhere. There is no analytics on this route,
      no third-party script, no assistant and no browser-agent tool
      registration. The worker that does the arithmetic has no way to
      reach the network at all, which a test over the built bundle
      checks.</li>
      <li>The one inherited thing that still loads code here is the site's
      own search, and only if you open it: pressing <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>{' '}
      fetches a static index from this origin. It reads nothing from this
      page and writes nothing.</li>
      <li>It needs no account, and there is nothing to buy.</li>
      <li>Dispose releases the pack, and every later calculation is refused until you load one again.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <script type="module" src="/precision-preview/app.mjs"></script>
</Base>

<style>
  .pp-lede { font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 62ch; }
  .pp-warn-box { margin: 1.4rem 0; }
  .pp-warn-box p { margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .pp-warn-box p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .pp-grid { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); }
  .pp-grid h3 { font-size: .95rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .pp-grid p { margin: 0; font-size: .9rem; }
  .pp-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .8rem; align-items: center; margin: 0 0 .8rem; }
  .pp-row label { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .45rem; font-size: .88rem; }
  .pp-row input[type=text] { font: inherit; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .82rem; padding: .4rem .55rem; width: 19ch; }
  .pp-row select { font: inherit; font-size: .85rem; padding: .35rem .5rem; }
  .pp-file-label input { font-size: .8rem; }
  .pp-state { font-size: .85rem; margin: .3rem 0; }
  .pp-ok { color: var(--ok, #6fcf97); }
  .pp-bad { color: var(--bad, #e86a5a); }
  .pp-warn { color: var(--warn, #e8b04b); font-size: .85rem; }
  .pp-dim { opacity: .68; }
  .pp-dl, .pp-claim { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: .15rem 1.1rem; font-size: .82rem; margin: .6rem 0; }
  .pp-dl dt, .pp-claim dt { opacity: .68; }
  .pp-dl dd, .pp-claim dd { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); overflow-wrap: anywhere; min-width: 0; }
  .pp { overflow-x: clip; }
  .pp-corrections ul { margin: .4rem 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: .82rem; }
  .pp-explain { font-size: .88rem; max-width: 66ch; margin: .6rem 0; }
  /* A wide table scrolls inside its own panel. Letting it widen the
     document instead is what made the whole page scroll sideways on a
     390px viewport. */
  .pp-out { overflow-x: auto; }
  .pp-out table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; min-width: 30rem; margin-top: .6rem; font-size: .8rem; }
  .pp-out th, .pp-out td { text-align: left; padding: .3rem .55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline, #232838); }
  .pp-out .num, .pp-out th.num { text-align: right; font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .pp-list { font-size: .88rem; max-width: 66ch; }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .pp-row { flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; }
    .pp-row input[type=text] { width: 100%; }
    .pp-dl, .pp-claim { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: .05rem; }
    .pp-out table { min-width: 26rem; }
    .pp-dl dt, .pp-claim dt { margin-top: .4rem; }
  }
</style>
